# fixhunt

**Hunts for linter autofixes that silently change what your program does. Any linter; your repo too.**

Every linter labels some of its automatic fixes "safe", and every CI pipeline that runs
`biome lint --write`, `ruff check --fix` or `clang-tidy -fix` trusts that label. The label is wrong
more often than you would think. fixhunt is an agent that reads a rule's documentation, writes small
programs that sit on the rule's semantic edge cases, applies the fix in a sandbox, and checks with
model-free oracles whether the program still does the same thing. Each hit is minimized, searched
against the upstream issue tracker, and written up as an evidence sheet for a human to file.

Two ways to use it. **Hunt** a rule: the agent writes adversarial programs for that rule and tests the
fix on them (how the findings below were made). **Scan** your repository: apply each safe fix to a copy
of your project and run your own test command before and after in a sandbox. Linters are JSON specs
(`tools/*.json`; Biome, ruff, ESLint and clang-tidy ship as examples), so nothing in the pipeline is tied
to a particular tool.

Built for the Nebius x NVIDIA Global AI Hackathon (Coding and Agentic Engineering track).
Nemotron models on Nebius Token Factory do the reading, writing and judging; the oracles never
involve a model.

## What it finds

| Oracle | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `fix-changes-behavior` | the program's stdout, exit status or thrown error differs after the fix |
| `fix-breaks-syntax` | the fixed file no longer parses or compiles |
| `fix-not-fixpoint` | the rule still fires on its own output, or the fix adds diagnostics |
| `tool-crash` | the linter panicked, timed out or exited abnormally |
| labelled safe | any of the above on a fix the tool itself calls safe |

## Results so far (2026-10-17)

Two sweeps over every rule whose fix the tool labels safe (Biome 2.5.15: 64 rules; ruff 0.17.1:
151 rules), about 3,400 generated programs, 130 oracle-backed findings, roughly $3 of inference.

| Status | Finding |
|---|---|
| filed | ruff FURB103: the safe fix drops the expression around `f.write()`, so a guarded write becomes unconditional ([astral-sh/ruff#29452](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/29452)) |
| candidate | ruff FURB129: `for line in fp.readlines()` → `for line in fp` changes behavior when the loop body reads from the file |
| candidate | Biome noUselessLoneBlockStatements: the fix splices the block body onto the previous line and breaks semicolon-less code |
| candidate | Biome noNegationElse: the fix emits invalid code for unbraced `if`/`else` |
| candidate | Biome useNumberNamespace: the fix ignores a shadowed `Number` binding and throws at runtime |
| rediscovered | Biome #11937, #11938, #11943, #11934, #11939, #11933, #11351; ruff #14011, #25182 |

Details, upstream searches and reproducers: [`docs/findings.md`](docs/findings.md),
[`docs/triage.md`](docs/triage.md), [`examples/`](examples/). Candidates are filed by a person after
re-verification, in their own words, following each project's AI policy.

## Quick start

```sh
bun install
cp .env.example .env            # NEBIUS_API_KEY, optional TAVILY_API_KEY
bun src/cli.ts hunt --tool biome --rule noUselessElse --n 8 --rounds 2
bun src/cli.ts hunt --tool ruff --rule FURB103
bun src/cli.ts check --tool biome --rule useObjectSpread --file examples/biome-11943-object-spread-proto.js --report
bun src/cli.ts scan --tool ruff --dir ~/myproject --run "pytest -q"   # your repo: which safe fixes change your tests?
bun src/cli.ts sweep --tool biome          # every Biome rule with a safe fix
bun src/cli.ts tools                       # tool specs loaded from tools/, $FIXHUNT_TOOLS, --tool-file
bun src/cli.ts serve                       # dashboard with live hunts at http://localhost:8731
bun src/cli.ts render                      # static export of the dashboard to runs/site/
bun src/cli.ts triage --out docs/triage.md # ranked findings table
```

The dashboard opens on the ranked findings. Each finding page shows the minimized program before
and after the fix side by side, both outputs, the judge's grade, the model's explanation, the repro
commands and the upstream search, with a button that re-runs the finding in a fresh Token Factory
Sandbox. The live hunt page runs a hunt against Token Factory and streams analysis, programs, oracle
hits and reports as they happen.

Requirements: [Bun](https://bun.sh), Node (runs Biome), `uv` (runs ruff), Python 3. Generated programs
run in [Token Factory Sandboxes](https://docs.tokenfactory.nebius.com/sandboxes/overview) when the key
has access (`FIXHUNT_SANDBOX=auto`), otherwise under `bwrap` locally.

### Sandboxes

Every execution of a generated program is a disposable Token Factory Sandbox: a fresh VM from a public
image (`node:20-slim`, `python:3.12-slim`, `gcc:14`), networking off, a timeout, and the working
directory shipped as a gzipped tarball on stdin, so there is no upload step and no image to prepare.
A run costs about a tenth of a cent and returns in one to two seconds; programs in a round are evaluated
in parallel. Delta-debugging minimization needs up to 60 evaluations per finding, so it runs under
`bwrap` locally and the minimized program is confirmed in a sandbox before it is judged and reported.
Sandbox runs are logged to `runs/spend.jsonl` next to model calls and shown on the dashboard.

## How it works

```
rule ──► ANALYZE (Nemotron 3 Super) ──► firing shapes, silent shapes, what the fix rewrites, rule-specific hazards
      ──► GENERATE (Nemotron 3 Super) ──► N self-checking programs, one per hazard; round 2 gets feedback
      ──► RUN      (Token Factory Sandboxes) ──► run twice (determinism) · lint · apply fix · parse check · run · re-lint
      ──► ORACLES  (no model)          ──► crash · syntax · behavior diff · fixpoint · "safe" label mismatch
      ──► MINIMIZE (ddmin, oracle as test, before-run shape as invariant)
      ──► JUDGE    (Nemotron 3 Ultra)  ──► real · intended-change · introspection-only · program-bug, with the rule docs
      ──► DEDUP    (GitHub search + Tavily, Nemotron 3 Ultra triage)
      ──► REPORT   (evidence sheet: repro, commands, diff, observed, upstream matches)
```

### Where Nemotron and Token Factory are used

| Stage | Model | Why this one |
|---|---|---|
| Analyze, generate, dedup queries, explanation | `nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b` | follows a precise brief, writes valid programs |
| Judge, dedup triage | `nvidia/Nemotron-3-Ultra-550b-a55b` | grades findings against the rule docs; strict same-defect judgement over issue titles and snippets |
| Minimize proposal | `nvidia/Nemotron-3_5-Lightning` | proposes a shorter repro before delta debugging; the oracle accepts or rejects it |

All calls go through the OpenAI-compatible Token Factory endpoint with thinking disabled for
structured stages. A full 16-program hunt with reports costs well under a cent; spend is logged
per call in `runs/spend.jsonl`.

### Judge calibration

The judge is advisory, so it is measured. `data/judge-labels.json` holds the hand triage of 89 findings
(30 real, 29 documented rewrites, 12 introspection-only, 18 broken programs or environment artefacts);
`bun src/cli.ts judge-eval` re-grades them and prints the confusion matrix.

| Judge | Accuracy | Precision on "real" | Recall on "real" | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| v1: rule docs only | 57% | 53% | 100% | |
| v2: + mechanical hints (introspection and environment patterns, pre-existing failure), ordered grades, worked examples | 79% | 71% | 97% | $0.16 |
| v3: + the "rule exists because the builtin can be shadowed" contrast | 87% | 86% | 97% | $0.18 |

Most remaining disagreements are programs that already failed before the fix, which the judge grades
"program-bug" and the rule-level labels do not.

### Trust rules

- Only oracle-backed findings get an evidence sheet. The judge's grade is advisory and shown as such.
- Both versions of a program run under the same file name, twice, with no network; non-deterministic or
  non-terminating programs are rejected before the fix is applied.
- Every finding ships with the minimized program, exact tool version, commands, and before/after output.
- Nothing is posted anywhere. A human re-verifies, checks the duplicates, and writes the upstream issue
  in their own words, following each project's AI policy.

## Any linter: tool specs

A tool is a JSON file that says how to print the version, look up a rule and its fix label, lint one file
for one rule, apply the fix, syntax-check and run a program. The engine (`src/tools/spec.ts`) turns the spec
into the adapter the pipeline uses; no code in the pipeline names a tool. See [`docs/tool-spec.md`](docs/tool-spec.md).

| Spec | Notes |
|---|---|
| `tools/biome.json` | fix label per rule from `biome explain`; catalog helper reads the configuration schema |
| `tools/ruff.json` | fix label per diagnostic from `--output-format json`; catalog from `ruff rule --all` |
| `tools/eslint.json` | written from the spec alone as the generality test; fixable rules count as safe per ESLint's fix policy |
| `tools/clang-tidy.json` | docs from a local llvm-project checkout or GitHub; binaries via `CLANG_TIDY`, `CLANGXX`, `CXXFLAGS`; compiles and runs inside `gcc:14` |

Add your own to `tools/`, to a directory in `FIXHUNT_TOOLS`, or pass `--tool-file`.

## Scan your repository

```sh
bun src/cli.ts scan --tool ruff --dir ~/myproject --run "pip install -q pytest && pytest -q" [--rules A,B] [--limit k]
```

For every rule the tool labels safe, `scan` applies that rule's fix to a copy of the project, diffs it, and
if anything changed runs your command again in a fresh sandbox (network on, so dependencies can install).
A finding is a rule whose fix changed the command's output or exit status; the report
(`runs/<scan>/scan.md`) carries the diff and both outputs. The command is run twice on the unmodified
project first; if it is not deterministic the scan stops rather than guess.

## Layout

```
src/cli.ts        hunt · check · sweep · reprocess · refresh · triage · judge-eval · serve · render
src/contree.ts    Token Factory Sandboxes client (disposable VM per run)
src/gen.ts        analyze + generate prompts, hazard catalogs, static screens
src/oracles.ts    the per-program pipeline and oracles
src/minimize.ts   ddmin with the oracle as the test
src/judge.ts      docs-aware grading of each finding
src/dedup.ts      GitHub + Tavily search, triage
src/report.ts     evidence sheet
src/pipeline.ts   minimize → judge → dedup → report, with stored evidence
src/catalog.ts    rule catalogs for sweeps (data/*.json)
src/server.ts     dashboard server and live-hunt event stream
src/web/          data model, HTML components, pages, static export
src/tools/spec.ts engine that turns a JSON tool spec into an adapter; registry.ts loads tools/*.json
src/scan.ts       scan mode: safe fixes applied to a repository, tests before and after
tools/            tool specs (biome, ruff, eslint, clang-tidy) and their helper scripts
data/curated.json hand-verified findings with their upstream status
runs/             artifacts per hunt (results.json, per-program files, reports/)
```

