# Tool specs

fixhunt drives any linter, formatter or codemod through a JSON spec. The pipeline (oracles, minimizer,
judge, dedup, dashboard, sweeps, scan) never knows which tool it is running. Built-in specs live in
`tools/*.json`; add your own there, in a directory listed in `FIXHUNT_TOOLS` (colon-separated), or pass
`--tool-file my-tool.json` on the command line. `bun src/cli.ts tools` lists what is loaded.

A spec needs: how to print the version, how to look up a rule (its id, docs and fix label), how to lint one
file for one rule and parse the diagnostics, how to apply that rule's fix, how to syntax-check a file, and how
to run a program. Optional: a catalog command for sweeps, sweep filters, repro command templates, a sandbox
image.

## Fields

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `id`, `name` | tool id used on the command line and in run ids |
| `language`, `ext` | language id for hazard catalogs and static screens (`js`, `py`, `cpp`, or any other id; unknown ids get generic hazards) and the file extension for generated programs |
| `repo` | GitHub `owner/name` searched by the dedup stage |
| `sandboxImage` | Token Factory Sandboxes image the program runs in (`node:20-slim`, `python:3.12-slim`, `gcc:14`, ...) |
| `docsFence` | code-fence language for evidence sheets |
| `runnerHint` | how a program is run, shown to the generator; defaults to the run command |
| `vars` | named values usable as `{NAME}` in commands: `env` (environment variable), `candidates` (first binary found on PATH), `default`, `split` (expands to several argv entries) |
| `env` | environment for every tool command |
| `version` | `cmd`, optional `regex` (first group) and `stripPrefix` |
| `rule` | `cmd` prints information about one rule; `idFromOutput` regex normalizes the id from that output; `split` is a regex with named groups available as `{rule.<group>}`; `upper` upper-cases the input; `fixKind` reads the label with `regex` + `map` (`default`, `onError`); `docs` fetches documentation from `file` or `url` templates, with `prefix` and `fallback` |
| `lint` | `cmd` with `{rule}` and `{file}`; `parse` is `json` (`items` path, `rule`, `message`, `severity`, `line`/`col` or `span`, `fix` + `fixMap`, `fixPresent` + `fixPresentKind`, `rulePrefix`, `nonJsonOk`) or `regex` (named groups `rule`, `message`, `line`, `col`, `severity`); `crash` has `okExit` and `stderrRegex` |
| `fix` | `cmd`; `unsafeArgs` are inserted before the file argument when unsafe fixes are requested |
| `check` | syntax or type check command; non-zero exit means the fixed file no longer parses |
| `run` | `cmd` (isolated: sandbox or bwrap); optional `pre` (local compile step), `sandboxCmd` (alternative command inside the sandbox image, e.g. compile and run), `env`, `timeoutMs`, `image`; `{exe}` is the file name with `.bin` |
| `repro` | command templates for the evidence sheet (`{unsafe}` expands to the unsafe args) |
| `catalog` | `cmd` printing JSON; `parse` with `items`, `rule`, `name`, `fixKind` or `fixKindFrom` (`field`, `map` of regex to kind, `default`), `note`, `languages`, `skipIf` |
| `sweep` | `fixKinds` (default `["safe"]`), `unsafeFixKinds`, `languages`, `skipRuleRegex`, `requireNoteRegex` |

Paths are dot-separated (`location.row`, `0.messages`); `$` is the root. Fix kinds are `safe`, `unsafe`,
`none`, `unknown`. A per-diagnostic label (ruff, ESLint) overrides the rule-level one.

## Minimal example

```json
{
  "id": "mytool", "language": "py", "ext": "py", "repo": "me/mytool", "sandboxImage": "python:3.12-slim",
  "version": { "cmd": ["mytool", "--version"] },
  "rule": { "cmd": ["mytool", "explain", "{rule}"], "fixKind": { "regex": "^fix: (\\w+)", "default": "unknown" } },
  "lint": { "cmd": ["mytool", "lint", "--rule", "{rule}", "--json", "{file}"], "parse": { "format": "json", "items": "$", "rule": "rule", "message": "msg", "line": "line" } },
  "fix": { "cmd": ["mytool", "lint", "--rule", "{rule}", "--fix", "{file}"] },
  "check": { "cmd": ["python3", "-m", "py_compile", "{file}"] },
  "run": { "cmd": ["python3", "{file}"] }
}
```

Helper scripts are fine where a tool has no CLI for something (see `tools/biome.catalog.ts`,
`tools/eslint.rule.ts`): the spec calls them like any other command.
# fixhunt — project plan (v2, 2026-10-17)

Working name: **fixhunt** (was diaghunt; rename freely). Entry for the Nebius x NVIDIA Global AI
Hackathon, Coding and Agentic Engineering track. Rules in `docs/hackathon.md`, option survey in
`docs/options-2026-10-17.md`, the earlier LLVM-only plan in `docs/archive/project-2026-10-12.md`.

Deadline **2026-10-30 10:00 PDT**. 13 days. No code yet.

## One-line pitch

An agent that hunts for linter autofixes and formatter rewrites that silently change what a
program does, across ruff, Biome, ESLint and clang-tidy, and turns each hit into a verified,
minimized, de-duplicated bug report with the evidence attached.

## Why this is general, and why now

- Every autofix tool labels fixes "safe" and every CI pipeline that runs `--fix`, `--write`
  or `-fix` trusts that label. The label is wrong often enough to matter:
  - ruff: 500 issues ever carrying the `fixes` label, 76 open today, recent titles include
    "safe fixes that cause runtime errors" (#26922) and "fixes can change runtime behaviour" (#24603).
  - Biome: one contributor filed ~35 rule bugs in Sept 2026, about 15 of them "safe fix is not
    safe" (#11933–#11944), most already fixed by maintainers. Each report used the same recipe:
    small program, run before and after the fix, show the diff or the `tsc` error. The reporter
    disclosed that Claude Code helped build the reproductions. That recipe is what fixhunt automates.
  - clang-tidy: 737 issues mention fix-its. Three of the author's own Sept–Oct 2026 LLVM issues
    are semantics-changing or crashing fix-its, so clang-tidy is the "deep" backend.
- Prior art is narrow: llvm-hackme fuzzes LLVM middle-end PRs with Alive2 as the oracle;
  csmith/yarpgen target compilers. Nothing public targets autofix safety across linters with
  a behavioral oracle. No academic paper on it surfaced in a search either.
- The Nemotron role is real, not decorative: reading a rule's docs and source to write programs
  that sit on its semantic edge cases is exactly what the Biome reporter did by hand.

## Audiences (the "Potential Impact" criterion)

1. Teams running autofix in CI or pre-commit: a finding tells them which rules to demote to
   "unsafe" before the fix lands in their code.
2. Linter and formatter maintainers: verified, minimized reports in their issue template.
3. Rule authors: stress-test a new rule before release (PR-triggered mode, stretch goal, same
   shape as llvm-hackme but for linters).

## Pipeline

```
 tool + rule(s) + version (ruff | biome | eslint | clang-tidy)
        |
 [1] TARGET    Nemotron 3.5 Lightning reads the rule docs/source, lists semantic hazards
        |      (hoisting, getters, __proto__, interface merging, var-in-loop, arguments.length,
        |      numeric coercion, comments, macros ...) and emits N self-checking programs,
        |      each with a predicted verdict (should fire / should not fire)
        v
 [2] RUN       sandbox per program: lint → apply fix → re-lint; run program before and after
        |      Token Factory Sandboxes (branch per program, prepared image per tool version),
        |      Docker locally as the baseline
        v
 [3] ORACLES   model-free, in order of strength:
        |      O1 tool crash / panic / timeout
        |      O2 fixed output fails to parse / compile / type-check (python -m py_compile, tsc, clang)
        |      O3 behavior diff: stdout / exit code / exception before vs after the fix
        |      O4 fixpoint: rule fires again after its own fix, or a new diagnostic appears;
        |         formatter output not idempotent
        |      O5 label mismatch: fix marked "safe" and O2/O3 failed  → headline finding class
        |      predicted verdict ≠ observed, with no oracle hit → "disputed", goes to the judge
        v
 [4] JUDGE     Nemotron 3 Ultra, disputed cases only: false positive / false negative / tool is right
 [5] MINIMIZE  delta-debug with the oracle as the test; Lightning proposes cuts, oracle confirms
 [6] DEDUP     Tavily search + GitHub issue search for the rule name and symptom  (Tavily bonus)
 [7] REPORT    Nemotron 3 Super drafts the issue in the project's template, evidence attached;
               draft only, a human files it, with an AI-assistance disclosure line
        v
 [8] SERVE     hosted dashboard: pick tool/rule/version → hunt → findings feed with before/after
               diff, oracle evidence, severity, draft issue; pre-populated for judges
```

### Model routing

| Stage | Model | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Target / generate | `nvidia/Nemotron-3_5-Lightning` | volume, $0.06/$0.24 per 1M, structured JSON |
| Minimize proposals | Lightning | many cheap rounds, oracle decides |
| Judge disputed | `nvidia/Nemotron-3-Ultra-550b-a55b` | semantic reasoning, small filtered set |
| Report | `nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b` | prose quality at moderate cost |

### Trust rules (the part maintainers care about)

- Only oracle-backed findings (O1–O5) become draft reports. Judge-only findings are shown as
  "needs a human" and never drafted.
- Every finding ships with: minimized program, exact tool version, command lines, before/after
  output. Reproducible by copy-paste.
- Nothing is posted automatically. Disclosure line in every draft.

## Backends (order of implementation)

| Tool | Why | Runner cost |
|---|---|---|
| Biome (JS/TS) | single binary, safe/unsafe labels, 15 fresh ground-truth bugs, playground links | trivial |
| ruff (Python) | single binary, `--fix --unsafe-fixes`, 76 open fix issues | trivial |
| clang-tidy (C++) | author's expertise, own ground truth, `-fix` + compile oracle | container with clang-tools |
| ESLint | ubiquitous, slower to set up, lower bug density | last, if time |

Ground truth for the demo: pin Biome 2.5.14 and the ruff version from the FURB103 report,
rediscover 2–3 fixed bugs (e.g. Biome #11943 `useObjectSpread` `__proto__`, #11944
`noUselessStringConcat` number stringification), then hunt the current releases for new ones.

## Requirement mapping

| Requirement | How |
|---|---|
| Runtime Token Factory call | stages 1, 4, 5, 7 |
| NVIDIA open model | Lightning, Super, Ultra |
| Track fit | agent writes programs, runs tools, tests outputs, drafts the fix report |
| Demo URL (live to 2026-12-15) | hosted dashboard, pre-populated findings, live hunt button |
| Public repo + licence | MIT, licence detected by GitHub |
| README | one Docker image, `NEBIUS_API_KEY`, one command |
| Video < 3 min | script below |
| Feedback section | Lightning JSON adherence, Sandboxes beta experience, Ultra latency |
| Tavily bonus | dedup stage makes a functional runtime Tavily call |

